#show: docs-subchapter.with(
  title: [[13.1]: Western Expansion],
  route: "west1",
)

The lifestyle of the native Americans may be described as the typical nomadic tribes who lives on buffaloes. They followed the herds of buffalo across the plains, relying on them for food, clothing, and shelter. This way of life was deeply connected to the land and the natural resources available to them. The tribes are tied together by familial blood. 

The discovery of gold in Colorado in 1858 drew tens
of thousands of miners to the region.


== The Government Restricts Native Americans

#lbl(<evt:fort-laramie-1851>, event(
  "Treaty of Fort Laramie / Horse Creek Treaty (1851)",
)[The United States negotiated with Plains nations to recognize territorial boundaries and secure passage along emigrant trails. Signatories agreed to peaceful passage in exchange for payments and protection of their lands. ])

#lbl(<cuz:fort-laramie-1851>, cause[Growing emigrant traffic competed with Native communities for grass, game, and wood. The government sought to keep western routes open while reducing conflict.])

Recognizing boundaries did not resolve the competing uses of the land. A treaty's ability to reduce tension depended on whether its promises were enforced, rather than on its existence alone.

#lbl(<evt:sand-creek>, event(
  "Sand Creek Massacre (November 29, 1864)",
)[Colonel John Chivington's Colorado troops attacked a Cheyenne and Arapaho encampment whose leaders had sought peace and believed they were under military protection. The National Park Service estimates that about 230 people were killed, mostly women, children, and elderly people. ])

#lbl(<cuz:sand-creek>, cause[Western expansion reduced Native hunting grounds, while territorial officials and military officers failed to secure peace. Chivington's decision to attack turned this wider conflict into a particular massacre; pressure over land alone does not explain or excuse that choice.])

This episode suggests a mechanism by which violence can deepen conflict: attacking people who have sought protection makes later promises of protection less credible. This is an interpretation of the event, rather than a claim that every subsequent conflict was inevitable.

#lbl(<evt:fetterman>, event(
  "Fetterman Fight / Battle of the Hundred Slain (December 21, 1866)",
)[During Red Cloud's War, Lakota, Cheyenne, and Arapaho warriors ambushed a force led by Captain William J. Fetterman near Fort Phil Kearny on the Bozeman Trail. All 81 men in his force were killed. ])

#lbl(<cuz:fetterman>, cause[The Bozeman Trail and its army forts intruded into Native hunting territory. Red Cloud's resistance challenged the government's ability to maintain the route. ])

#lbl(<evt:fort-laramie-1868>, event(
  "Treaty of Fort Laramie (1868)",
)[The peace settlement closed the Bozeman Trail and provided for abandonment of its forts. It established the Great Sioux Reservation, including the Black Hills, and retained hunting rights beyond the reservation. Sitting Bull did not sign or accept the agreement. ])

#lbl(<cuz:fort-laramie-1868>, cause[Native military resistance made continued occupation costly and brought the United States back to negotiation. Different Lakota leaders nevertheless disagreed over whether reservation life was an acceptable settlement.])

The closure of the trail shows that resistance could change government policy. However, a concession at one moment did not guarantee lasting respect for Native sovereignty: after gold was found in the Black Hills, miners entered the protected land, and the United States confiscated it in 1877. 

#lbl(<evt:red-river>, event(
  "Red River War (1874–1875)",
)[The U.S. Army campaigned against southern Plains nations, forcing remaining resistance onto reservations. At Palo Duro Canyon on September 28, 1874, Colonel Ranald S. Mackenzie's troops burned lodges and winter supplies and captured about 1,400 horses, killing most of them. ])

#lbl(<cuz:red-river>, cause[Conflict over southern Plains land and reservation confinement became a campaign aimed at destroying the resources needed to remain outside reservations. The destruction of horses and winter stores deprived families of mobility and subsistence.])

The campaign illustrates how destroying the means of continuing resistance can be decisive even without defeating every warrior in battle. This is an interpretation of the campaign's mechanism, not a justification of its methods.

#lbl(<evt:black-hills-gold>, event(
  "Black Hills Gold Rush (from 1874)",
)[Custer's 1874 expedition confirmed gold in the Black Hills, drawing miners into land protected by the 1868 treaty. The army initially tried to exclude them, but in late 1875 President Grant ordered it to leave the miners alone. ])

#lbl(<cuz:black-hills-gold>, cause[Gold increased the economic incentive to enter Lakota land. The government's abandonment of enforcement allowed that incentive to override treaty protections.])

This event extends the earlier analysis of economic interests: a new opportunity for profit changed the pressure on government policy. However, the policy reversal remained a political choice; the existence of gold did not itself require treaty violations.

== Sitting Bull: How Much Can One Person Change History?

#lbl(<evt:little-bighorn>, event(
  "Battle of the Little Bighorn (June 25–26, 1876)",
)[Lakota and Cheyenne forces defeated Custer's command. Custer and the five companies under his immediate command were killed, while soldiers with Reno and Benteen survived; the entire Seventh Cavalry was not destroyed. Sitting Bull, a Hunkpapa Lakota political and spiritual leader, helped inspire resistance through his leadership and Sun Dance vision; he did not fight in the battle. ])

#lbl(<cuz:little-bighorn>, cause[Gold seekers encroached on the Black Hills despite treaty protections. The government ordered Native people outside reservations to return, and military action followed their refusal. ])

As with Hutchinson and Franklin earlier, consider both the individual and the strong undercurrents carrying him. Sitting Bull did not create the conflict over land, and removing him from the story would not remove that conflict. Yet this does not make him interchangeable with any other leader. His refusal to accept the 1868 settlement and his spiritual authority offered followers a focus for continued resistance. His contribution was to help turn shared grievances into collective action; the military victory still depended on many warriors and leaders.

The comparison suggests that one person's influence depends partly on whether others can act through that person. Hutchinson's official position and Franklin's reputation placed them differently within the imperial conflict. Sitting Bull's authority similarly depended on followers who recognized it. This is an analytical comparison, not proof that their circumstances or choices were equivalent.

#lbl(<evt:sitting-bull-end>, event(
  "Sitting Bull's Surrender (1881) and Death (1890)",
)[After hardship in Canada and the departure of many followers, Sitting Bull surrendered at Fort Buford on July 20, 1881. On December 15, 1890, he was killed during an attempted arrest by Lakota agency police at Standing Rock, ordered amid official fears concerning the Ghost Dance. ])

#lbl(<cuz:sitting-bull-end>, cause[Military pursuit and scarcity restricted the means of sustaining resistance. Later, the reservation agent treated Sitting Bull's continuing influence as a threat.])

His eventual surrender shows the limits of personal authority when followers cannot obtain the resources needed to continue. However, defeat does not mean that his actions had no historical effect. A leader may alter the timing, organization, and cost of resistance without determining its final outcome. The evidence supports substantial influence on collective resistance, but it cannot establish what would have happened without him. Sitting Bull therefore illustrates constrained agency: individuals help shape events, while their available choices and the durability of their achievements depend on wider material and political conditions.

== Loss of Land and Subsistence

#lbl(<evt:bison-destruction>, event(
  "Destruction of the Bison (nineteenth century, especially the 1870s)",
)[North America's bison population fell from an estimated 30–60 million to fewer than 1,000 by the 1890s. This collapse devastated Plains communities that relied on bison for food, clothing, shelter, and other necessities. The initial population is an estimate, rather than an exact count. ])

#lbl(<cuz:bison-destruction>, cause[Commercial demand for hides, rail transport, and rifles enabled mass slaughter. Military support for destroying Native subsistence, together with disease, drought, and competition from livestock, also contributed. ])

The destruction of bison helps explain why military victories could fail to preserve independence. Resistance required an enduring source of food and supplies, as well as capable leaders and warriors.

#lbl(<evt:dawes>, event(
  "Dawes Act (February 8, 1887)",
)[Congress authorized the division of communally held reservation lands into individual allotments and the sale of remaining land to settlers. Allotment and later extensions of the policy caused extensive losses of Native land. ])

#lbl(<cuz:dawes>, cause[Federal policy sought assimilation through individual landownership and farming, weakening communal tenure and tribal organization. Many allotments were unsuitable for agriculture, and many recipients lacked the resources to farm them. ])
This policy shows that dispossession could proceed through changes in property law as well as military force. Its stated promise of individual ownership must be assessed alongside its effects on collective control of land.

== The Ghost Dance and Wounded Knee

#lbl(<evt:ghost-dance>, event(
  "Ghost Dance Movement (1889–1890)",
)[The religious movement associated with the Northern Paiute prophet Wovoka spread among Native communities, including the Lakota. It offered hope of renewal amid dispossession and hardship. Officials interpreted its spread as a threat, contributing to the crisis in which Sitting Bull was killed during his attempted arrest. ])

#lbl(<cuz:ghost-dance>, cause[Loss of land and subsistence made promises of restoration compelling. Official fear of renewed resistance turned a religious movement into a focus of coercive intervention.])

#lbl(<evt:wounded-knee>, event(
  "Wounded Knee Massacre (December 29, 1890)",
)[After intercepting Spotted Elk (Big Foot) and his Lakota band on December 28, the Seventh Cavalry held them at Wounded Knee Creek. During disarmament the next morning, a shot was fired and troops opened fire, including with Hotchkiss guns. Hundreds of Lakota were killed, including women and children. Accounts differ about the initial shot and the exact death toll. ])

#lbl(<cuz:wounded-knee>, cause[The army's response to the Ghost Dance crisis brought a detained Lakota community into a heavily armed disarmament operation. The immediate outbreak of gunfire occurred within this wider setting of fear and coercion.])

Alongside @evt:sand-creek, this massacre illustrates the danger of treating Native communities as collective military threats. The wider conflict shaped the circumstances, but decisions about arrest, detention, disarmament, and firing remained actions taken by particular people.
